Context

Looper needs to finish independent tasks concurrently without requiring
project-specific configuration or exposing worktree management to users.

Decision

Keep the Bash command interface and agent adapters. Use a Python standard
library coordinator for task planning, isolated worktrees, process control,
verification, and durable state. Publish verified changes as unstaged edits
from internal Git snapshots so the user's branch and index stay intact.

Verify speculative integration candidates concurrently and publish them in
order only when the tested base is still current. Retain failed work for
repair or recovery. Keep --jobs 1 as the sequential fallback.

Alternatives

Shared-directory workers risk concurrent edits. Independent branches with
manual integration add user work. A full command rewrite increases the
scope without improving the existing agent adapters.

Consequences and validation

Parallel execution requires Python 3.9 or later and a supported Git root.
The coordinator owns backlog updates and uses repository locks and a
publication journal. Existing checks and 31 new tests cover scheduling,
Git integration, preservation of local edits, and recovery. Tests use local
agent stubs; real-project speed improvements remain unmeasured.
